  "notes": "The LA-pop producer who moved into Nashville — keyboard-first harmony under country instrumentation. The band is stated as a deliberate choice: \"BPM 70–110 straight on 2&4 — a deliberately narrow, slow band.\" The harmony is the entry's real claim to distinctness and it is explicitly not a country vocabulary: \"his productions use IV–iv I chromatic borrows and static I7 vamps alongside I–V–vi–IV, which is a pop/R&B vocabulary rather than a country one,\" and he was \"trained as a jazz trombonist and pianist from age seven,\" so the seventh and ninth shares run far above the genre's triad-heavy default and the IV–iv–I borrow is a family. The method is quoted as an absolute: \"piano first, always — Rhodes/Wurlitzer and organ carry the harmony,\" which is why the chord voicing is close and low rather than guitar-shaped, and why the counter is a pad and a gospel stack rather than a steel lick. Drums are \"a hip-hop-derived pocket — programmed clap/snap on the backbeat, 16th hats, a short sampled kick on 1 and the 'and-of-3,' tambourine on chorus 8ths, and live cymbal overdubs. Half-time verses opening to straight choruses,\" the last of which is the `fullTimeVariantProb`. Melodies are \"groove-driven with real melisma — an outlier in country.\" ⚠ The synth sub sits with \"an electric bass with R&B syncopation; the bass is written as a melodic voice, not a root generator,\" so no `drums.bass808` block is authored and `followRootsProb` is the lowest of any producer here. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the clap offsets, the full-time variant share and the counter ratio.",
